bash script exit codes :
exit code is writted by bash for every command executed in terminal.this code can be used to display error messages when there is any problem while running the script

when a command executed successfully bash returns 0 as exit code, for any error while executing the the command, non zero value 1 to 255 is returned based on error type

an exit status (or exit code) is a numeric value between 0 and 255 that a process returns to its parent shell upon completion. It serves as a direct way for a command to report whether it executed successfully or encountered an error

You can check the exit status of the most recently executed command by printing the special shell variable $?:
echo $?
for successful commands it will return 0


Exit Code	Meaning	   Description


0	Success -> The command completed perfectly without any errors.
1	General error -> Catch-all for miscellaneous errors, such as "permission denied" or dividing by zero.
2	Misuse of shell built-ins -> Missing arguments or syntax errors in a built-in shell command.
126	Command invoked cannot execute -> The command was found, but you lack the permissions required to run it.
127	Command not found -> You typed a command that does not exist or isn't in your PATH.
128	Invalid argument to exit -> The exit command was given a value outside the valid 0–255 integer range.

128 + N	Fatal signal error -> If a process is forced to terminate by a system signal N, its exit code becomes 128 + N. (e.g., terminating a script with Ctrl+C sends signal 2, resulting in exit code 130).





you can also use exit code as part of your script

after script interprets and processes all its content, the script process exits and passes the control back to parent process that called it

you can use exit command to immediately leave the script 

you can use the exit command with an optional integer argument b/w 0 to 255, which represents an exit code

ex: exit 0
